{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Good cause eviction - criminal conviction and civil liability causes","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found liable in a civil proceeding for, assault, terrorist threats, theft or illegal drug activity on the premises, or found in a civil action to have committed human trafficking. A three-day Notice to Quit is required.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Evictions for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order for listed causes (holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, rule or lease violations). Three months' notice is required for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month otherwise.","key_value":"3-month or 1-month notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Any building with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless it meets a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"units","role":"covered","op":"at_least","n":2}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house if it does not meet one (1) of the exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act (N.J.S.A. 55:13B-3)."]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts to personally occupy a unit must occupy it at least six months; one who evicts to permanently board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6-month owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal - civil rights of tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, makes a good-faith health or safety complaint, joins a tenant organization, or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction - 90-day notice to quit","requirement":"Before evicting a tenant after foreclosure, the landlord must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit when the buyer wants to personally occupy the property as a primary residence; the notice may be given 90 days before the lease expires.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law giving tenants longer periods or additional protections.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans, and lived there at least one year before conversion, may be protected from eviction for 40 years if income-qualified.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income up to 3x county average per-person income or $50,000","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet age, disability or veteran criteria and lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant must be at least 62 before the conversion recording, or permanently disabled, or a qualifying disabled veteran discharged at 60% or higher, and have lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"Extends protected-tenancy eviction protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","key_value":"Hudson County","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; currently only Hudson County qualifies.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","n_rules":8,"note":null}

